ranger wrote:
>
> It's a game that I was tempted to quit, since I didn't mean to start
> a Wizard and this one had really crummy starting stats and nothing
> in the starting equipment to speak of.  Basically this Wizard started
> her mission as a wimpy "fighter-mage", and did the protection racket.

Starting out squib-like makes sense.  I've tried a few squib games but
have never managed them later.  Casting gets more and more tempting as
the game proceeds.

> It was touch-and-go quite a few times getting to minetown, as it
> always is in these games.  At least five separate fights got her
> to one HP, but she made it, and minetown was coaligned.  At this
> point, I started to get interested in the game.  There was nothing
> really memorable in minetown.  I think the most interesting thing
> was a tinning kit, seriously, a very few un-id scrolls (not ID),
> and some food.

A tinning kit is one of my favorite wizard finds when the mine town is
coalgined - As a wizard I want to go through the regular watchmen for
their gloves and because there's usually at least one wearing a studded
leather armor.  Having a tinning kit means if my pet does not survive to
take them all out I can do it myself then get my telepathy back.

> After that, the game got pretty boring.  She was altar camping and a
> random black pudding was dumb enough to show up at her altar, so I
> went ahead and took up the RNG's offer, plenty of spellbooks and
> an accidental crowning - oh well.

No luck getting enough books first to get Stormy at crowning.  You ended
up with good enough backup melee weapon anyways.

> Famine had the Chaotic altar, and Wyvern the Wizard wished up a
> Ki-Rin (named Coffee) and a blessed fireproof saddle, to ride from
> the Astral Plane in style.

Very cool!

>   D - a blessed rustproof +5 helm of brilliance (being worn)
>   E - a blessed rustproof +5 pair of gauntlets of power (being worn)
>   M - a blessed greased fireproof +5 robe (being worn)

Nice way to offset the effects of the gloves.

>   a blessed rustproof +7 pick-axe              <-- I dug out quite a bit 
> of the dungeon, just out of boredom

The plus speeds digging.  It's hard to tell except by writing down the
counter number and comparing.  Pus seven?  Did you manage that first
try?  I don't think a pick axe can be dulled by engraving.

>   7 Nazguls		<-- I guess I should keep count

It's fun to keep the rings in the bag and number them to keep track.


